WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.500
Ninety-nine percent of all
freshwater ice on Earth

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.740
is sitting on top of
Greenland and Antarctica,

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.130
and each year, a little more
of it melts into the ocean.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.200
Normally, it would take
hundreds to thousands of years

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.260
for it all to melt away.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.930
But what if something happened

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.593
that caused a massive
global melt overnight?

00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:27.563
As we slept, sea levels would rise

00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:30.910
by a whopping 66 meters.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.460
Coastal cities like New
York, Shanghai, and London

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.130
would drown in the apocalyptic mass flood,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.980
forcing up to 40% of
the world's population

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.820
out of their homes.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.420
While all this chaos ensues aboveground,

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.090
something equally sinister
is happening below.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.017
All that rising salt water will infiltrate

00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:56.660
groundwater reserves farther inland,

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.580
forcing its way into
nearby freshwater aquifers.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.770
You know, the ones that
supply our drinking water,

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.440
irrigation systems, and
power-plant cooling systems?

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.020
All those aquifers would be destroyed.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.850
Not good.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.850
On top of that, the ice on
Greenland and Antarctica

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.420
is made of fresh water, so when it melts,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.450
that's about 69% of the
world's freshwater supply

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.100
that's going straight into the oceans.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.490
This will wreak havoc on our

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.160
ocean currents and weather patterns.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.460
Take the Gulf Stream, for example.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.280
It's a strong ocean current

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.143
that brings warm air to northern Europe

00:01:36.143 --> 00:01:39.310
and relies on dense, salty
water from the Arctic

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.970
in order to function.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.240
But a flood of fresh water
would dilute the current

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.960
and could weaken or
even stop it altogether.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.868
Without that warm air,

00:01:49.868 --> 00:01:52.801
temperatures in northern
Europe would plummet,

00:01:52.801 --> 00:01:55.410
and that could spawn a mini ice age,

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.880
according to some experts.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.530
That's not even the worst of it.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.170
Take a look at what will happen

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.410
when that last 1% of freshwater ice

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:09.100
that's not part of Greenland
or Antarctica thaws.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.920
Some of that 1% is sitting
in glaciers farther inland.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.180
The Himalayan glaciers specifically

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.130
pose one of the largest threats

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.756
because of what's trapped inside:

00:02:19.756 --> 00:02:23.498
toxic chemicals like
dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane,

00:02:23.498 --> 00:02:25.222
or DDT.

00:02:25.222 --> 00:02:27.790
Scientists discovered
that glaciers like this

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.180
can store these chemicals for decades.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.860
But as they thaw, those
glaciers release the chemicals

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.040
into rivers, lakes, and
groundwater reserves,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.040
poisoning each one as they go.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.830
The rest of that 1% is
hanging out underground,

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.455
mostly in the Arctic tundra,

00:02:47.455 --> 00:02:50.496
as something called permafrost.

00:02:50.496 --> 00:02:52.920
Permafrost is organic matter that's been

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.260
frozen in the ground for two-plus years.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:58.910
Now, one of the most immediate problems

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.710
with thawing permafrost
would be mercury poisoning.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:07.180
That's right: There are an
estimated 15 million gallons

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.490
of mercury stored up in
the Arctic permafrost.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.840
That's almost equal to
the amount of mercury

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.400
everywhere else on Earth.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.550
On top of that, the organic
matter in permafrost

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.650
is a tasty meal for microorganisms.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.180
After they digest it all, they fart out

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.750
two of the most potent
greenhouse gases out there,

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.460
carbon dioxide and methane.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.850
Scientists estimate this could double

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:37.620
the current levels of greenhouse
gases in the atmosphere,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.460
and potentially cause
global temperatures to rise

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:44.630
by 3.5 degrees Celsius compared to today.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.360
That might not sound like much,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.550
but say goodbye to that
mini European ice age,

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.890
and even rivers and
lakes around the world.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.370
They'd evaporate from
the higher temperatures

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.450
and cause mass droughts
and desert-like climates.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:02.340
And all that extra water
vapor in the atmosphere

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.760
would fuel more frequent and stronger

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.890
storms, floods, and hurricanes.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:12.087
So all of that newly established
coastline on the eastern US

00:04:12.087 --> 00:04:15.362
would be one of the last
places you'd want to live.

00:04:15.362 --> 00:04:20.200
Instead, there would be mass
migrations to Canada, Alaska,

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:24.140
the Arctic, and even what's
left of the Antarctic.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:28.270
And you're right, this is
probably never going to happen.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.830
After all, there's enough ice right now

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.850
to cover the entire
continent of North America

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.703
in a sheet a mile thick.

00:04:36.703 --> 00:04:39.780
So the next time you hear
about record-breaking heat

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.260
or ultra-powerful hurricanes,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.640
at least you know that it could be worse.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.450
But scientists estimate
that if we don't take action

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:52.820
and global temperatures increase
by just 1 degree Celsius,

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.153
the effects of climate
change we already see today

00:04:56.153 --> 00:04:58.480
will be irreversible.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.340
So yes, it could be worse,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:04.003
and it will be if we're not careful.

